/* ============================================================================
limit-of-sinx-over-xv.epher -- sin(xv)/xv at tiny xv rounds to 1

The classic squeeze-theorem limit sin(xv)/xv -> 1 as xv -> 0 (Stewart,
"Calculus", 8th ed., sec. 2.6) probed numerically at xv = 1e-8, both
signs. The ratio misses 1 by about xv^2/6 = 1.7e-17 there, far below
the calculator's precision, so it prints exactly 1; at xv = 1e-4 the
deficit xv^2/6 = 1.7e-9 shows up in the last printed digits instead.

The same limit defines the derivative of sin xv at 0, and the builtin
derivative (a numeric central difference, not symbolic) agrees.

Accuracy: in exact arithmetic sin(xv)/xv at xv = 1e-8 is 1 - 1.7e-25;
every value below rounds at about the 12th printed digit.

Run it from a terminal:
  Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/calculus/limits/limit-of-sinx-over-xv.epher
  Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\calculus\limits\limit-of-sinx-over-xv.epher"
  macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/calculus/limits/limit-of-sinx-over-xv.epher
The knob to edit is `xv` below.
============================================================================ */

// ---- the knob ---------------------------------------------------------------
const xv = 1e-8

// ---- sin(xv)/xv at xv = 1e-8 and -1e-8: both print the limit, 1 ----------------
sin(xv)/xv
sin(-xv)/(-xv)
abs(sin(xv)/xv - 1) < 1e-12

// ---- at xv = 1e-4 and 1e-6 the xv^2/6 deficit is (partly) visible -------------
for t in {1e-4, 1e-6} do sin(t)/t

// ---- cross-check: derivative of sin at 0, on a fresh variable t -----------
// (xv is bound by the knob above, so a bound xv would make the expression
// constant; t is free).
derivative(sin(t), 0)

/* ---- expected output ---------------------------------------------------------
= 0.00000001
= 1
= 1
= true
= {0.999999998333, 1}
= 1
*/
